1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,513
A mobile operator in Baku signs two hundred new subscribers. Each one

2
00:00:04,613 --> 00:00:08,725
picks a tariff: Basic at ten manat a month, Standard at twenty,

3
00:00:08,825 --> 00:00:13,739
or Premium at thirty-five. How uncertain is next month's revenue? To answer

4
00:00:13,839 --> 00:00:17,349
that, we need the counts in all three tariffs at once.

5
00:00:19,433 --> 00:00:21,502
That is the multinomial distribution: the

6
00:00:21,602 --> 00:00:23,512
binomial, with more than two outcomes.

7
00:00:24,600 --> 00:00:28,981
Picture each subscriber as a ball dropping into one of three

8
00:00:29,081 --> 00:00:34,657
bins, independently, with the same probabilities every time: one half, point

9
00:00:34,757 --> 00:00:38,989
three five, and point one five. Here are the first twenty.

10
00:00:39,089 --> 00:00:42,201
Y one, Y two and Y three count the balls in

11
00:00:42,301 --> 00:00:45,711
each bin, and together they always add up to n.

12
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:49,774
The probability of a particular set of counts has two factors,

13
00:00:49,874 --> 00:00:53,849
just as in Chapter two. One ordered sequence with these counts

14
00:00:53,949 --> 00:00:56,938
has probability p one to the y one, times p two

15
00:00:57,038 --> 00:00:59,765
to the y two, times p three to the y three.

16
00:00:59,865 --> 00:01:04,168
And the coefficient counts how many orderings give the same counts.

17
00:01:05,266 --> 00:01:11,250
Now a shortcut. If you only care about Basic, merge the other two bins

18
00:01:11,350 --> 00:01:17,420
into one: not Basic. Every subscriber is now a success or a failure, so

19
00:01:17,520 --> 00:01:22,113
Y one on its own is just binomial. Its mean is n p one

20
00:01:22,213 --> 00:01:27,501
and its variance n p one q one. The same holds for every cell.

21
00:01:28,600 --> 00:01:31,830
With two hundred subscribers, we expect one hundred

22
00:01:31,930 --> 00:01:35,030
on Basic, seventy on Standard, thirty on Premium.

23
00:01:36,116 --> 00:01:39,250
Now simulate two hundred and fifty months, and plot

24
00:01:39,350 --> 00:01:42,548
the Basic count against the Premium count. The cloud

25
00:01:42,648 --> 00:01:45,211
tilts downward. That is not a coincidence.

26
00:01:45,300 --> 00:01:49,525
The bins share a fixed total. A subscriber who picks Premium is one

27
00:01:49,625 --> 00:01:53,464
who did not pick Basic. So when one count is high, the others

28
00:01:53,564 --> 00:01:57,983
tend to be low, and every pair of cells has negative covariance: minus

29
00:01:58,083 --> 00:02:01,082
n p s p t. For Basic and Premium, minus fifteen.

30
00:02:03,166 --> 00:02:06,151
Back to revenue. R is ten Y one plus twenty

31
00:02:06,251 --> 00:02:09,811
Y two plus thirty-five Y three. Its mean needs only

32
00:02:09,911 --> 00:02:14,116
the cell means: three thousand four hundred and fifty manat.

33
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:18,069
The spread is different. Treat the three counts as unrelated, and

34
00:02:18,169 --> 00:02:22,343
the standard deviation comes out at about two hundred and thirty-three

35
00:02:22,443 --> 00:02:26,373
manat. Include the negative covariances, and it drops to about one

36
00:02:26,473 --> 00:02:30,526
hundred and twenty-three. The counts move against each other, so the

37
00:02:30,626 --> 00:02:33,091
revenue swings much less than you'd think.

38
00:02:34,183 --> 00:02:38,835
So: a multinomial experiment drops n independent trials into k cells, and

39
00:02:38,935 --> 00:02:41,959
the counts add up to n. Each count on its own is

40
00:02:42,059 --> 00:02:46,515
binomial. And because the total is fixed, any two counts have negative

41
00:02:46,615 --> 00:02:49,965
covariance, which you must keep when you add them up.

